  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 1238 PM MDT Fri Oct 2 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA westerly to ever so slightly northwesterly flow aloft is in place \nto close out the work week. A weak wave progressing through this \nflow through Saturday morning has a low chance to eek out a shower \nor two across the plains and vicinity. Otherwise, the remainder of \nthe weekend and next week looks warm and dry as upper level \nridging moves in. \n\nA slightly more brisk mid level flow will result in a breezy day \nover the plains through the remainder of the day today and again on \nMonday. Monday looks to be the warmest day overall through the \nupcoming week as well, which will raise concerns for some elevated \nfire weather conditions in areas that have been a bit drier \nrecently. \n\nGuidance suggests some upper level troughing finally moves in very \nlate next week or next weekend. Given this is a week out, confidence \nremains low in any specifics.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nA few high resolution members want to develop a few showers across \nCentral MT this afternoon. The members that do produce showers \nmaintain dew points in the mid 40s, even after mixing out. Should \ndew points remain elevated (Low probability) after winds develop at \nthe surface, I cannot rule out a few showers through the evening. \nThe most likely scenario will be for falling dew points as mixing \noccurs, which would put a significant damper on any instability.\n\nOtherwise attention turns to Monday, where anomalously warm \ntemperatures will combine with dry and breezy conditions. While most \nareas have seen sufficient precipitation recently to stave off \nsignificant fire weather concerns, elevated fire weather conditions \nwill be around in locally drier areas. The probability for exceeding \nthe 90th percentile GEFS-based HDWI is around 50% across much of the \nplains on Monday, particularly in areas east of Great Falls. That \nsaid, fuels do not look to be overly receptive at this time. -AM",
